## KISS Principle

## What It Means

KISS — **Keep It Simple, Stupid** — is a design heuristic coined in the US Navy in the 1960s. The idea: systems perform best when their design is simple. Complexity is a liability.

## Why Complexity Hurts

```
- Every abstraction layer is a place for bugs to hide
- Onboarding new engineers takes longer
- Debugging becomes exponentially harder
- Over-engineered code is harder to change safely
```

## Practical Examples

```typescript
// ❌ Over-engineered — 40 lines to do what a filter can
class ProductFilterStrategy {
  constructor(private predicate: (p: Product) => boolean) {}
  execute(products: Product[]) { return products.filter(this.predicate); }
}
const inStock = new ProductFilterStrategy(p => p.stock > 0);
const result = inStock.execute(products);

// ✅ KISS
const inStock = products.filter(p => p.stock > 0);
```

```typescript
// ❌ Deeply nested conditions
function getDiscount(user: User) {
  if (user) {
    if (user.isPremium) {
      if (user.loyaltyYears > 5) {
        return 0.3;
      } else {
        return 0.2;
      }
    } else {
      return 0.1;
    }
  }
  return 0;
}

// ✅ Early returns — flat and readable
function getDiscount(user: User | null): number {
  if (!user) return 0;
  if (!user.isPremium) return 0.1;
  return user.loyaltyYears > 5 ? 0.3 : 0.2;
}
```

## KISS vs Other Principles

| Principle | Focus |
|-----------|-------|
| KISS | Avoid unnecessary complexity |
| YAGNI | Don't build things you don't need yet |
| DRY | Avoid code duplication |

All three point in the same direction: write less, simpler code that solves the actual problem.

## Test Driven Development (TDD)

## The Cycle

```
1. RED   → Write a failing test for the next small piece of behaviour
2. GREEN → Write the simplest code that makes the test pass
3. REFACTOR → Clean up without breaking the test

Repeat for every new behaviour.
```

## Example

```typescript
// 1. RED — test doesn't compile yet
describe('formatPrice', () => {
  it('formats a number as GBP', () => {
    expect(formatPrice(9.99)).toBe('£9.99');
  });

  it('rounds to 2 decimal places', () => {
    expect(formatPrice(9.999)).toBe('£10.00');
  });
});

// 2. GREEN — minimal implementation
export function formatPrice(amount: number): string {
  return `£${amount.toFixed(2)}`;
}

// 3. REFACTOR — extract currency symbol, add locale support, etc.
export function formatPrice(
  amount: number,
  currency = 'GBP',
  locale = 'en-GB'
): string {
  return new Intl.NumberFormat(locale, {
    style: 'currency',
    currency,
  }).format(amount);
}
```

## Benefits

```
✅ Forces API design before implementation
✅ Built-in regression suite
✅ Encourages small, focused units of code
✅ Confidence to refactor
✅ Documents expected behaviour
```

## Limitations

```
❌ Slower initial pace (especially for exploratory work)
❌ Hard to apply to UI rendering, animations, legacy code
❌ Can lead to over-mocking if misapplied
❌ Tests can become brittle if written at wrong abstraction level
```

## When TDD Shines

```
- Pure business logic / domain rules
- Utility functions
- API response transformations
- Bug fixes (write a failing test reproducing the bug first)
```

## Fail Early / Fail Fast

## Core Idea

When something is wrong, make the system **scream immediately** — don't let it limp on in an invalid state and fail mysteriously later.

## Examples

```typescript
// ❌ Silent failure — the bug manifests somewhere downstream
function processOrder(order: Order | null) {
  const total = order?.items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0) ?? 0;
  // continues with total = 0, creates a $0 order silently
}

// ✅ Fail early — guard at the entry point
function processOrder(order: Order) {
  if (!order) throw new Error('processOrder requires a valid order');
  if (order.items.length === 0) throw new Error('Order has no items');
  const total = order.items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0);
  return total;
}
```

```typescript
// TypeScript strict mode — fail at compile time
// tsconfig.json: "strict": true
function getUser(id: string): User {
  // compiler forces you to handle the null case
  return db.find(id) as User; // ← TS will warn if return type doesn't match
}
```

## When to Fail Early

```
✅ Input validation at service/function boundaries
✅ Configuration checks at application startup
✅ Assertions in critical business rules
✅ Type guards when data comes from external sources (APIs, DB)
✅ CI pipeline — fail on type errors, lint errors, test failures before deploy
```

## Fail Fast vs Robust Design

```
Fail Fast: crash/error immediately on invalid state
  → Good for internal logic, dev environments, domain invariants

Robust:    handle errors gracefully, continue if possible
  → Good for external I/O, user-facing APIs, third-party integrations

Both are valid — use the right one in the right place.
```

## Lower Layers Must Not Know About Higher Layers

## The Layered Architecture Rule

```
┌─────────────────────────┐
│   Presentation (UI)     │  ← knows about Application
├─────────────────────────┤
│   Application / Use Cases│  ← knows about Domain
├─────────────────────────┤
│   Domain (Business Logic)│  ← knows about nothing above it
├─────────────────────────┤
│   Infrastructure (DB, IO)│  ← implements Domain interfaces
└─────────────────────────┘

Arrows point DOWN only. Never up.
```

## Why This Matters

```
1. Testability
   Domain logic can be unit-tested with no HTTP, no database.
   If Domain imports a React hook, you can't test it without a browser.

2. Reusability
   The same Domain layer can be consumed by a REST API, a CLI,
   a background worker, or a GraphQL layer — without modification.

3. Replaceability
   Swap React for a CLI? The Domain doesn't care.
   Swap PostgreSQL for MongoDB? Only the Infrastructure layer changes.

4. Single source of change
   A UI redesign only touches Presentation.
   A business rule change only touches Domain.
```

## Violation Example

```typescript
// ❌ Domain layer importing a UI concern
import { useRouter } from 'next/navigation'; // WRONG in domain

class OrderService {
  async cancelOrder(id: string) {
    await this.repo.cancel(id);
    const router = useRouter(); // Domain knows about routing — NEVER
    router.push('/orders');
  }
}
```

```typescript
// ✅ Presentation handles navigation; domain returns a result
class OrderService {
  async cancelOrder(id: string): Promise<void> {
    await this.repo.cancel(id);
    // done — let the caller decide what to do next
  }
}

// In the component:
await orderService.cancelOrder(id);
router.push('/orders'); // navigation stays in Presentation
```

## Clean Architecture

## The Rings

```
         ┌─────────────────────────────────┐
         │       Frameworks & Drivers       │  ← Web, DB, UI
         │   ┌─────────────────────────┐   │
         │   │   Interface Adapters    │   │  ← Controllers, Presenters
         │   │  ┌───────────────────┐  │   │
         │   │  │   Use Cases       │  │   │  ← Application business rules
         │   │  │  ┌─────────────┐  │  │   │
         │   │  │  │  Entities   │  │  │   │  ← Enterprise business rules
         │   │  │  └─────────────┘  │  │   │
         │   │  └───────────────────┘  │   │
         │   └─────────────────────────┘   │
         └─────────────────────────────────┘

         Dependency Rule: arrows point INWARD only.
```

## The Dependency Rule

Source code dependencies **must point only inward**. Nothing in an inner ring can know anything at all about something in an outer ring.

```typescript
// ✅ Domain entity — knows nothing about DB or HTTP
export class User {
  constructor(
    public readonly id: string,
    public readonly email: string,
  ) {}

  isAdmin(): boolean {
    return this.email.endsWith('@company.com');
  }
}

// ✅ Use case — depends on repository interface (inner), not implementation
export class GetUserUseCase {
  constructor(private userRepo: UserRepository) {} // interface

  async execute(id: string): Promise<User> {
    const user = await this.userRepo.findById(id);
    if (!user) throw new UserNotFoundError(id);
    return user;
  }
}

// ✅ Infrastructure — implements the interface (outer depends on inner)
export class SupabaseUserRepository implements UserRepository {
  async findById(id: string): Promise<User | null> {
    const { data } = await supabase.from('users').select().eq('id', id).single();
    return data ? new User(data.id, data.email) : null;
  }
}
```

## Benefits

```
✅ Testable without DB/HTTP
✅ Framework-agnostic domain layer
✅ Easy to swap infrastructure
✅ Business rules are the stable core; frameworks are volatile details
```

## The Model in DDD

## What It Is

A DDD model is a **simplified, purposeful representation** of a business domain. It captures:

```
- The key concepts (entities, value objects)
- The rules that govern them (invariants, business logic)
- The language used to describe them (ubiquitous language)
- How they interact (aggregates, domain events, services)
```

The model is NOT:
```
❌ A database schema (though it influences one)
❌ A UI wireframe
❌ A class diagram of every real-world object
```

## Example: Booking Domain Model

```typescript
// Entity — has identity
class Booking {
  constructor(
    public readonly id: BookingId,
    private guestId: GuestId,
    private roomId: RoomId,
    private period: DateRange, // Value Object
    private status: BookingStatus,
  ) {}

  confirm(): BookingConfirmed {
    if (this.status !== 'pending') throw new InvalidOperationError();
    this.status = 'confirmed';
    return new BookingConfirmed(this.id, new Date());
  }
}

// Value Object — no identity, immutable
class DateRange {
  constructor(
    public readonly checkIn: Date,
    public readonly checkOut: Date,
  ) {
    if (checkOut <= checkIn) throw new Error('checkOut must be after checkIn');
  }

  nights(): number {
    return Math.ceil((this.checkOut.getTime() - this.checkIn.getTime()) / 86400000);
  }
}
```

## The Model Evolves

```
Initial model → conversations with domain experts →
discover new concepts → refine → code changes →
better model → repeat

This is the 'knowledge crunching' at the heart of DDD.
```

## Repository Pattern vs Service Layer

## Repository: Data Access Abstraction

```typescript
// Interface (defined in domain layer)
export interface UserRepository {
  findById(id: string): Promise<User | null>;
  findByEmail(email: string): Promise<User | null>;
  save(user: User): Promise<void>;
  delete(id: string): Promise<void>;
}

// Implementation (lives in infrastructure layer)
export class SupabaseUserRepository implements UserRepository {
  async findById(id: string): Promise<User | null> {
    const { data } = await supabase
      .from('users').select().eq('id', id).single();
    return data ? toDomain(data) : null;
  }
  // ...other methods
}
```

## Service Layer: Business Workflow Orchestration

```typescript
export class UserService {
  constructor(
    private userRepo: UserRepository,
    private emailService: EmailService,
  ) {}

  async registerUser(email: string, name: string): Promise<User> {
    // 1. Check invariant
    const existing = await this.userRepo.findByEmail(email);
    if (existing) throw new EmailAlreadyUsedError(email);

    // 2. Create domain entity
    const user = User.create({ email, name });

    // 3. Persist
    await this.userRepo.save(user);

    // 4. Side effects
    await this.emailService.sendWelcome(user);

    return user;
  }
}
```

## Key Distinction

```
Repository  → How to get/store data (infrastructure concern)
Service     → What to do with data (application/business concern)

Service USES repository.
Repository does NOT know about services.

❌ Antipattern: SQL in a service
❌ Antipattern: Business rules in a repository
```

## SOLID Principles

## S — Single Responsibility Principle

```typescript
// ❌ One class does too much
class User {
  save() { /* DB logic */ }
  sendWelcomeEmail() { /* Email logic */ }
  renderProfile() { /* UI logic */ }
}

// ✅ One responsibility each
class UserRepository { save(user: User) {} }
class EmailService { sendWelcome(user: User) {} }
class UserProfileComponent { render(user: User) {} }
```

## O — Open/Closed Principle

```typescript
// ❌ Must modify existing class to add a new payment method
class PaymentProcessor {
  process(type: 'card' | 'paypal') {
    if (type === 'card') { /* ... */ }
    if (type === 'paypal') { /* ... */ }
  }
}

// ✅ Open for extension via interface
interface PaymentMethod { charge(amount: number): Promise<void>; }
class CardPayment implements PaymentMethod { async charge(amount) {} }
class PayPalPayment implements PaymentMethod { async charge(amount) {} }
// Add new payment method: implement the interface. Don't touch existing code.
```

## L — Liskov Substitution Principle

```typescript
// ❌ Square breaks the contract of Rectangle (setWidth changes both dimensions)
class Square extends Rectangle {
  setWidth(w: number) { this.width = w; this.height = w; } // violation
}

// ✅ Don't extend when the subtype changes the parent's contract
```

## I — Interface Segregation Principle

```typescript
// ❌ Fat interface forces all implementors to define unused methods
interface Animal { walk(): void; swim(): void; fly(): void; }

// ✅ Focused interfaces
interface Walkable { walk(): void; }
interface Swimmable { swim(): void; }
interface Flyable { fly(): void; }
class Duck implements Walkable, Swimmable {}
```

## D — Dependency Inversion Principle

```typescript
// ❌ High-level depends on low-level concretion
class OrderService {
  private db = new MySQLDatabase(); // tightly coupled
}

// ✅ Depend on abstraction
class OrderService {
  constructor(private db: Database) {} // interface injected
}
```
